      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mt-3 mb-4">2026 State EV Incentives &mdash; Complete Guide to Electric Vehicle Rebates &amp; Tax Credits</h1>
      <div class="text-sm text-silver-500">Published January 25, 2026 &middot; 10 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-dark">
      <p><em>Last verified October 22, 2026 on official state program websites.</em> The federal EV tax credits (up to $7,500 new, $4,000 used) ended for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025, according to the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/clean-vehicle-tax-credits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS</a>. That makes state, utility and local programs the main source of EV savings in 2026. Many run in funding rounds and pause when money runs out, so confirm on the official site before you buy.</p>

      <h2>Biggest State EV Incentives Verified for 2026</h2>

      <h3>Maine &mdash; Up to $7,000 for Income-Qualified Buyers</h3>
      <p><a href="https://www.efficiencymaine.com/electric-vehicle-incentives-for-low-and-moderate-income/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Efficiency Maine</a> offers low-income buyers $7,000 for a new EV or $3,000 for a used one, and moderate-income buyers $5,000 new or $2,000 used, paired with an off-peak charger.</p>

      <h3>Massachusetts &mdash; $3,500 to $6,000 (MOR-EV)</h3>
      <p><a href="https://mor-ev.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MOR-EV</a> pays $3,500 for new battery EVs with an MSRP up to $55,000 and for income-eligible used EVs, plus $1,500 MOR-EV+ for income-qualified residents and a $1,000 trade-in bonus.</p>

      <h3>Connecticut &mdash; Up to $4,500 (CHEAPR)</h3>
      <p><a href="https://portal.ct.gov/deep/air/mobile-sources/cheapr/cheapr---home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CHEAPR</a> raised its new BEV rebate to $1,500 on October 15, 2026 ($500 for plug-in hybrids). Income-qualified Rebate+ buyers can reach $4,500 on a new BEV.</p>

      <h3>New Jersey &mdash; Up to $4,000 (Charge Up)</h3>
      <p><a href="https://chargeup.njcleanenergy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charge Up New Jersey</a> gives $1,500 at the dealer, or $4,000 total with income-qualified Charge Up+, plus up to $250 for a home Level 2 charger. See our <a href="/states/new-jersey/">New Jersey vehicle guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>Illinois &mdash; $2,000 to $4,000</h3>
      <p>The <a href="https://epa.illinois.gov/topics/ceja/electric-vehicle-rebates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Illinois EPA EV rebate</a> cycle runs August 1 to December 31, 2026: $2,000, or $4,000 for low-income applicants, applied for within 180 days of purchase. See our <a href="/states/illinois/">Illinois vehicle guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>Colorado &mdash; $750 to $3,250 State Tax Credit</h3>
      <p>Colorado's credit dropped to $750 for 2026 for new EVs with an MSRP up to $80,000, plus an extra $2,500 for EVs under $35,000 (<a href="https://energyoffice.colorado.gov/transportation/grants-incentives/electric-vehicle-tax-credits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colorado Energy Office</a>). Dealers can apply it at purchase. See our <a href="/states/colorado/">Colorado vehicle guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>New York &mdash; $500 to $2,000 (Drive Clean)</h3>
      <p>NYSERDA's <a href="https://www.nyserda.ny.gov/drive-clean-rebate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Drive Clean Rebate</a> is taken off the price at participating dealers, based on the model's range and MSRP.</p>

      <h3>California &mdash; Income-Qualified Programs</h3>
      <p>The statewide CVRP rebate closed on November 8, 2023. Income-qualified Californians who scrap an older car can apply for <a href="https://cleancars4all.arb.ca.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clean Cars 4 All</a> before buying, and many California utilities offer charger rebates. See our <a href="/states/california/">California vehicle guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>Oregon &mdash; Limited Window</h3>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.oregon.gov/deq/aq/programs/pages/zev-rebate.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oregon Clean Vehicle Rebate</a> only covers purchases from August 25 to November 4, 2026, and funds are running low; late applications may be waitlisted until spring 2027.</p>

      <h2>Programs Paused or Ended</h2>
      <p>Maryland's EV excise tax credit has no current funding (waiting list only). Minnesota's EV rebate funds are exhausted, Texas's 2026 light-duty EV round is closed, and Washington's instant rebate is fully claimed; its EV sales tax exemption expired July 31, 2025. New programs may open, so check before you buy.</p>

      <h2>Every Other State</h2>
      <p>For all other states, and for city, county and utility programs, search the U.S. Department of Energy's <a href="https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/state" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alternative Fuels Data Center</a>, then check your <a href="/states/">state guide</a> for registration fees and EV rules.</p>

      <h2>EV Registration Fees: The Hidden Cost</h2>
      <p>While many states encourage EV purchases, most states now also charge an annual fee for electric vehicles (and often plug-in hybrids) to replace lost gas-tax revenue. These fees are added on top of standard registration and vary widely by state, and several states adjust them each year for inflation. For the current amount in your state, check the National Conference of State Legislatures&rsquo; <a href="https://www.ncsl.org/energy/special-fees-on-plug-in-hybrid-and-electric-vehicles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tracker of special EV fees</a> or your state DMV, then factor the fee into your total cost of ownership.</p>

      <h2>Utility and Charging Incentives</h2>
      <p>Many electric utilities offer incentives independent of state programs. These can include rebates of $250&ndash;$1,000 for Level 2 home charger installation, discounted off-peak electricity rates for overnight EV charging, free or reduced-cost public charging on the utility's network, and smart charging programs that provide credits for allowing the utility to manage charging during peak demand. Contact your local utility company or visit their website to learn about available programs.</p>

      <h2>How to Maximize Your Savings</h2>
      <p>Stack every available incentive: first, check your <a href="/states/">state’s programs</a> and confirm funding is open. Second, ask your electric utility about charger rebates and EV rates. Third, check city or county programs. Fourth, compare automaker cash-back and financing offers. Finally, if you signed a binding contract and paid on or before September 30, 2025, you can still claim the federal credit on IRS Form 8936.</p>

      <h2>Checking a Specific Vehicle</h2>
      <p>State programs set their own vehicle rules, usually an MSRP cap, a minimum electric range or a battery-electric-only requirement. Use our <a href="/vin-decoder/">VIN Decoder</a> to confirm a vehicle’s exact model, trim, fuel type and assembly plant before you apply.</p>

      <h2>Looking Ahead</h2>
      <p>State EV incentive programs are evolving rapidly. Several states are considering new or expanded programs, while others are adjusting their EV registration fees. As EV adoption grows and gas tax revenues decline further, expect more states to implement EV-specific fees while simultaneously offering purchase incentives to accelerate the transition. Bookmark this page and check your <a href="/states/">state guide</a> regularly for the latest updates.</p>
